WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:03.909
Speaker 1: Today, we're going to be speaking
with Dr. Robert Waldinger, who's the director

00:00:03.909 --> 00:00:09.290
of the Harvard Study of Adult Development
and also coauthor of the new book The Good

00:00:09.290 --> 00:00:14.110
Life Lessons from the World's Longest Scientific
Study of Happiness.

00:00:14.110 --> 00:00:15.110
Really great to have you on.

00:00:15.110 --> 00:00:16.110
I appreciate your time.

00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:17.640
Speaker 2: Well, it's great to be here.

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:18.640
Thanks for having me.

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:23.140
Speaker 1: So one of the really interesting
things about this study, which started in

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:29.940
1938, is many studies on happiness are of
different groups of people, sometimes from

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:33.309
different countries across different time
periods.

00:00:33.309 --> 00:00:38.210
And it can become very difficult to really
compare apples to apples for, for lack of

00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:39.210
a better term.

00:00:39.210 --> 00:00:43.850
And one of the interesting things about this
study that now has gone on for more than eight

00:00:43.850 --> 00:00:48.680
decades is it followed the same individuals
and families.

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:54.320
It's very extensive, asked a ton of questions,
hundreds of measurements, includes bloodwork,

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:55.320
brain scans.

00:00:55.320 --> 00:01:01.120
I mean, you're really approaching this from
a sort of cover every aspect perspective.

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:05.360
Can you tell us just a little bit about how
this study started, the number of people that

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:09.180
were originally part of it and the number
that that are still with us today?

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:11.050
Speaker 2: Sure.

00:01:11.050 --> 00:01:16.280
That started as two studies that didn't know
about each other started on different parts

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:18.880
of the Harvard University campus.

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:25.540
One was a study begun at Harvard Health Service,
the student health Service with sophomores

00:01:25.540 --> 00:01:33.009
from Harvard College, and the other was a
study of delinquency, a study of delinquent

00:01:33.009 --> 00:01:39.760
and non delinquent boys from Boston's poorest
neighborhoods, from the most troubled families.

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:49.430
268 Harvard College undergraduates, 456 inner
city boys and all of them were studied as

00:01:49.430 --> 00:01:55.909
a way to understand thriving, how kids moved
from childhood into adulthood on good developmental

00:01:55.909 --> 00:02:02.600
paths, including the kids who were born with
so many strikes against them, you know, into

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:05.560
such disadvantaged circumstances.

00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:10.509
So it was radical for its time to be doing
these studies of human thriving.

00:02:10.509 --> 00:02:14.750
And then eventually my predecessor put the
two studies together and started looking at

00:02:14.750 --> 00:02:17.930
them as contrasting groups.

00:02:17.930 --> 00:02:26.080
Speaker 1: When we look at the popular literature
on happiness, there are all these often repeated

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:29.300
ideas or rules of thumb or whatever the case
may be.

00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:33.940
One of them is once you make $75,000 a year,
it's equivalent.

00:02:33.940 --> 00:02:37.390
When you get beyond that, you're not really
anymore.

00:02:37.390 --> 00:02:40.820
Sometimes the term is satisfied with your
life.

00:02:40.820 --> 00:02:43.030
Sometimes the term happy is used.

00:02:43.030 --> 00:02:47.090
Another one that you often hear is later in
life.

00:02:47.090 --> 00:02:52.349
Having spent more time in the office rarely
correlates to more happiness.

00:02:52.349 --> 00:02:56.030
Whereas having spent more time with family
and having more close relationships does.

00:02:56.030 --> 00:03:00.110
And, you know, then five years go by and there's
another study that comes out and it has a

00:03:00.110 --> 00:03:04.690
slightly different, different result on some
of those sort of like lowest hanging fruit

00:03:04.690 --> 00:03:06.670
things that are often discussed.

00:03:06.670 --> 00:03:08.269
What has this study determined?

00:03:08.269 --> 00:03:17.650
Speaker 2: Well, we determined that the inner
city group was no less happy than the privileged

00:03:17.650 --> 00:03:23.241
Harvard group, and that's more consistent
with other studies that say basically, once

00:03:23.241 --> 00:03:28.920
you get your basic economic needs met, then
making more and more money doesn't do it,

00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:31.340
doesn't increase your happiness.

00:03:31.340 --> 00:03:36.930
And that seems to be a pretty robust findings
across a lot of groups of people.

00:03:36.930 --> 00:03:40.980
Speaker 1: Is there something to that $75,000
number that you found?

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:44.069
Speaker 2: No, not 75,000 per say.

00:03:44.069 --> 00:03:49.120
And of course, it varies depending on whether
you live in the middle of New York City or

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:50.690
live in rural Iowa.

00:03:50.690 --> 00:03:51.690
Right.

00:03:51.690 --> 00:03:56.760
It's really the question of when, when you
have your basic needs met.

00:03:56.760 --> 00:04:02.150
Once you've gotten to that point in terms
of income, then more money doesn't do it for

00:04:02.150 --> 00:04:03.150
you.

00:04:03.150 --> 00:04:06.010
Then you can make millions more and on average,
people aren't happier.

00:04:06.010 --> 00:04:12.190
Speaker 1: There's this phrase nobody is on
their deathbed wishing they spent more time

00:04:12.190 --> 00:04:13.190
at the office.

00:04:13.190 --> 00:04:14.190
Right.

00:04:14.190 --> 00:04:15.250
You hear that very often?

00:04:15.250 --> 00:04:16.250
Yeah.

00:04:16.250 --> 00:04:18.280
What is the study have to say about that dynamic?

00:04:18.280 --> 00:04:23.800
Speaker 2: Well, we asked people when our
first generation folks were in their eighties.

00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:29.449
We asked them to look back on their lives
and we asked, what are you proudest of and

00:04:29.449 --> 00:04:31.810
what do you regret the most?

00:04:31.810 --> 00:04:35.280
And what they regretted the most, particularly
the men.

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:39.220
Because this was the World War two generation
they regretted the most.

00:04:39.220 --> 00:04:44.530
Spending too much time at work and not spending
enough time with the people they cared about.

00:04:44.530 --> 00:04:53.930
What they were proudest of was was to a person,
something to do with their relationships.

00:04:53.930 --> 00:05:00.110
So, you know, they didn't say I made a ton
of money or I won all these awards, you know,

00:05:00.110 --> 00:05:01.110
or I was famous.

00:05:01.110 --> 00:05:05.000
And we had people who were all of those things.

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:08.870
They said I was a good boss, I was a good
spouse.

00:05:08.870 --> 00:05:10.910
I was a good friend, a good parent.

00:05:10.910 --> 00:05:11.910
Right.

00:05:11.910 --> 00:05:16.699
So when they were looking back on what was
most meaningful, it wasn't about achievement.

00:05:16.699 --> 00:05:18.370
It was about connections.

00:05:18.370 --> 00:05:22.759
Speaker 1: Is it true that John F Kennedy
was one of the original participants?

00:05:22.759 --> 00:05:24.470
Speaker 2: It is true.

00:05:24.470 --> 00:05:30.720
And the only reason I can tell you that is
because he was outed by another journalist

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:32.669
about 15 years ago.

00:05:32.669 --> 00:05:39.680
Otherwise, we are bound to keep in confidence
the identities of all of the people in our

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:40.680
study.

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:45.370
So, you know, in fact, the book has stories
of real people woven all through the book.

00:05:45.370 --> 00:05:51.259
But the names and the superficial details
are disguised to make sure that we protect

00:05:51.259 --> 00:05:52.259
their privacy.

00:05:52.259 --> 00:05:58.490
Speaker 1: So it sounds like money beyond
a certain level, not a big contributor to

00:05:58.490 --> 00:06:02.380
happiness, fame, not a big contributor to
happiness.

00:06:02.380 --> 00:06:06.889
Having spent a lot of time at the office,
not not necessarily a contributor.

00:06:06.889 --> 00:06:08.569
Relationships of of different kinds.

00:06:08.569 --> 00:06:09.569
Yes.

00:06:09.569 --> 00:06:11.800
Family, friendships, etc..

00:06:11.800 --> 00:06:17.220
What else are the kind of positively correlated
stories that that you find?

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:20.820
Speaker 2: Taking care of your health matters
a lot.

00:06:20.820 --> 00:06:25.600
You know, it's it's like our grandmothers
could have told us that.

00:06:25.600 --> 00:06:31.599
But what we find and lots of studies find
is that, you know, getting regular health

00:06:31.599 --> 00:06:38.560
care, not smoking, not abusing the alcohol
and drugs, regular exercise, not becoming

00:06:38.560 --> 00:06:45.910
obese, all of those things make huge differences
in years of life lived and also in years of

00:06:45.910 --> 00:06:47.669
healthy life.

00:06:47.669 --> 00:06:52.060
So it really matters a lot to take care of
your health.

00:06:52.060 --> 00:06:57.370
The surprising thing for us was the finding
about relationships being so important, because

00:06:57.370 --> 00:07:02.199
at first we thought, well, how could relationships
actually get into your body and and change

00:07:02.199 --> 00:07:03.470
your physiology?

00:07:03.470 --> 00:07:08.470
And now for the last ten years we've been
studying that many other groups have, too,

00:07:08.470 --> 00:07:14.440
And we're understanding that this is a very
powerful, robust connection between relationships

00:07:14.440 --> 00:07:15.669
and physical health.

00:07:15.669 --> 00:07:18.379
Speaker 1: What about religion?

00:07:18.379 --> 00:07:24.039
Because there is religion can be a tough one
where it can be hard to separate the effect

00:07:24.039 --> 00:07:30.889
of being part of a community and the social
aspects versus the religiosity itself.

00:07:30.889 --> 00:07:34.069
And there's many, many studies on this.

00:07:34.069 --> 00:07:39.950
Did you find anything that would be sort of
clear in the data about religious participation?

00:07:39.950 --> 00:07:44.380
Speaker 2: Well, we asked people, do you have
a religious practice?

00:07:44.380 --> 00:07:46.970
Do you have a spiritual practice?

00:07:46.970 --> 00:07:49.940
And there were people who did and there were
people who did not.

00:07:49.940 --> 00:07:54.780
And when we compare those two groups, neither
group was, on average, happier.

00:07:54.780 --> 00:07:55.780
Hmm.

00:07:55.780 --> 00:08:00.669
So didn't make a difference in your happiness,
whether you were spiritual or religious or

00:08:00.669 --> 00:08:01.669
not.

00:08:01.669 --> 00:08:07.420
But many of the religious and spiritual people
said that during times of difficulty, their

00:08:07.420 --> 00:08:08.539
spiritual practice.

00:08:08.539 --> 00:08:11.090
It says we're helpful to that.

00:08:11.090 --> 00:08:15.280
Speaker 1: So that's more, I guess, qualitative
and quantitative.

00:08:15.280 --> 00:08:16.940
Speaker 2: Yeah.

00:08:16.940 --> 00:08:22.100
But quantitatively, when you compare the two
groups, no one group isn't happier.

00:08:22.100 --> 00:08:24.540
Speaker 1: What about travel?

00:08:24.540 --> 00:08:30.311
Because one of the things that's often written
about travel is because when you travel, you're

00:08:30.311 --> 00:08:33.710
not in your routine.

00:08:33.710 --> 00:08:39.930
Time seems to go by slower, which often one
of the things we read is that over time, you

00:08:39.930 --> 00:08:41.919
know, years go by very, very quickly.

00:08:41.919 --> 00:08:47.390
And part of this seems to be that there's
no cognitive difference day to day when you're

00:08:47.390 --> 00:08:49.420
kind of in your in your normal day to day
thing.

00:08:49.420 --> 00:08:51.470
So that travel is an interesting way.

00:08:51.470 --> 00:08:56.650
And when one might think there might be some
correlation to happiness as well there.

00:08:56.650 --> 00:08:58.050
Is there anything.

00:08:58.050 --> 00:09:02.410
Speaker 2: We did not study that specifically.

00:09:02.410 --> 00:09:05.010
But what you're saying makes a lot of sense.

00:09:05.010 --> 00:09:12.310
And and what we do find is that more cognitive
stimulation keeps us sharper over time, and

00:09:12.310 --> 00:09:14.620
that includes being connected with other people.

00:09:14.620 --> 00:09:21.540
So one of the effects we think of relationships
on keeping our brains healthy is that it stimulates

00:09:21.540 --> 00:09:22.540
us.

00:09:22.540 --> 00:09:24.220
People are always unpredictable.

00:09:24.220 --> 00:09:25.640
They're moving targets.

00:09:25.640 --> 00:09:29.700
What you're going to say to me next is something
I don't know, right?

00:09:29.700 --> 00:09:33.220
And that keeps my brain clicking on all cylinders.

00:09:33.220 --> 00:09:34.650
That's a good thing for the brain.

00:09:34.650 --> 00:09:38.670
And I think what you're pointing out is that
travel can do the same thing.

00:09:38.670 --> 00:09:42.480
That's why time seems to go more slowly during
travel because we're like on alert.

00:09:42.480 --> 00:09:44.240
Like, what am I going to see now?

00:09:44.240 --> 00:09:46.149
How do I find my way here?

00:09:46.149 --> 00:09:52.610
It's it's all of those challenges that we
think keeps us healthy cognitively as we get

00:09:52.610 --> 00:09:53.610
older.

00:09:53.610 --> 00:09:58.920
Speaker 1: One of the things that is it's
not unique to the United States, but it's

00:09:58.920 --> 00:10:05.200
certainly been written about a lot in the
United States, is the trend that people move

00:10:05.200 --> 00:10:11.060
relatively frequently in modern American society
much more commonly than in other parts of

00:10:11.060 --> 00:10:15.380
the world, don't live near their families,
nor where they grew up.

00:10:15.380 --> 00:10:19.730
I was recently reading Jared Diamond's book
about traditional societies, and he said,

00:10:19.730 --> 00:10:24.800
Well, a big difference there, aside from all
sorts of other differences, is that in more

00:10:24.800 --> 00:10:28.950
traditional societies, people tend to live
near where they grew up and near their family

00:10:28.950 --> 00:10:30.250
most of their lives.

00:10:30.250 --> 00:10:34.029
In the United States, it's increasingly uncommon.

00:10:34.029 --> 00:10:39.150
Was there something garnered from the study
related to that about those who kind of stay

00:10:39.150 --> 00:10:43.000
what we might call close to home versus those
that don't?

00:10:43.000 --> 00:10:49.990
Speaker 2: What we found was a difference
in the inner city group from the Harvard group.

00:10:49.990 --> 00:10:54.540
And that made a whole lot of sense because
the Harvard group was gathered from all over

00:10:54.540 --> 00:10:55.540
the country.

00:10:55.540 --> 00:11:01.950
Harvard College brings in undergrads from
everywhere in the U.S. and sometimes abroad.

00:11:01.950 --> 00:11:06.779
Unlike the inner Citigroup, where they were
chosen because they lived in Boston, their

00:11:06.779 --> 00:11:07.860
families lived in Boston.

00:11:07.860 --> 00:11:12.800
And what we found then in the next generation
was that, of course, more of the inner city

00:11:12.800 --> 00:11:17.200
group lived in this area, stayed near their
families.

00:11:17.200 --> 00:11:24.290
Now, what we have not done is an analysis
that compares are those second generation

00:11:24.290 --> 00:11:30.410
people in the inner city group happier then
more widely dispersed Harvard people?

00:11:30.410 --> 00:11:33.320
I expect that research exists.

00:11:33.320 --> 00:11:34.630
We just haven't done it.

00:11:34.630 --> 00:11:38.900
Speaker 1: Interesting in terms of the relationships.

00:11:38.900 --> 00:11:46.130
Lots of studies on friendship that look at
number of friends, but also quality of relationship.

00:11:46.130 --> 00:11:51.220
And what you see frequently repeated in popular
publications is it's not about having many

00:11:51.220 --> 00:11:59.339
friends as one ages, it's about having a few
very strong or rewarding relationships that

00:11:59.339 --> 00:12:03.230
really seems to correlate not only with happiness,
but sometimes even like a health claim will

00:12:03.230 --> 00:12:05.730
be made.

00:12:05.730 --> 00:12:06.870
What about that distinction?

00:12:06.870 --> 00:12:12.060
Speaker 2: Well, what we find is that, you
know, as with so many things, when you follow

00:12:12.060 --> 00:12:16.130
thousands of lives, one size never fits all.

00:12:16.130 --> 00:12:23.190
So we're all on a spectrum, temperamentally,
from introversion, shyness to extroversion.

00:12:23.190 --> 00:12:25.899
And most of us are somewhere in the middle.

00:12:25.899 --> 00:12:27.510
But there are extremes.

00:12:27.510 --> 00:12:33.899
And what we find is that shy people find lots
of people around them to be stressful.

00:12:33.899 --> 00:12:38.490
So actually having a lot of connections in
your life could be more stressful if you're

00:12:38.490 --> 00:12:40.149
temperamentally introverted.

00:12:40.149 --> 00:12:45.620
On the other hand, extroverted people get
their energy from people, so they may need

00:12:45.620 --> 00:12:47.370
a lot of people.

00:12:47.370 --> 00:12:53.740
So what we find is that it's a highly individual
matter and that each of us can really discern

00:12:53.740 --> 00:12:59.850
for ourselves what's the amount of connection
that works for me in my life and how can I

00:12:59.850 --> 00:13:02.930
work to have the amount that feels right to
me?

00:13:02.930 --> 00:13:04.350
Speaker 1: That's interesting.

00:13:04.350 --> 00:13:06.790
I mean, holding all else equal.

00:13:06.790 --> 00:13:10.870
Do you know whether introverts or extroverts
seem to be happier?

00:13:10.870 --> 00:13:13.480
Speaker 2: I don't know that.

00:13:13.480 --> 00:13:18.029
I do know that, for example, Susan Kane, some
of her work that there are people who work

00:13:18.029 --> 00:13:26.230
on this and I don't believe people have found
that introverts or extroverts are happier

00:13:26.230 --> 00:13:28.540
than the opposite extreme at all.

00:13:28.540 --> 00:13:35.700
I think one of the complicating factors is
that our culture glorifies extroversion.

00:13:35.700 --> 00:13:40.420
You know, being a party animal, right?

00:13:40.420 --> 00:13:45.700
You know, that's like an iconic character
in films and literature on social media.

00:13:45.700 --> 00:13:46.700
Right.

00:13:46.700 --> 00:13:51.880
And so that bias toward extroversion can make
introverted people feel like there may be

00:13:51.880 --> 00:13:53.000
something wrong with them.

00:13:53.000 --> 00:13:56.060
What we know is there isn't anything wrong
with them at all.

00:13:56.060 --> 00:13:58.170
It's perfectly normal, adaptive.

00:13:58.170 --> 00:14:04.050
Some of the most wonderful, most brilliant,
most talented people are shy.

00:14:04.050 --> 00:14:05.050
Not a problem.

00:14:05.050 --> 00:14:10.100
Speaker 1: The book is called The Good Life
Lessons from the World's Longest Scientific

00:14:10.100 --> 00:14:11.470
Study of Happiness.

00:14:11.470 --> 00:14:16.100
We've been speaking with the book's coauthor,
Dr. Robert Waldinger.

00:14:16.100 --> 00:14:17.870
Really appreciate your time and insights today.

00:14:17.870 --> 00:14:19.530
Speaker 2: Thank you for having me.

